<p>The United Arab Emirates said Tuesday it will leave OPEC and the wider OPEC+ alliance on May 1 after reviewing its production policy, current and future capacity and national interests, according to statements carried by WAM and accounts citing UAE Energy Minister Suhail Mohamed al-Mazrouei. The announcement removes one of the Gulf’s largest producers from the Saudi-led oil producers’ group as OPEC members prepare for a Vienna meeting and energy markets face disruptions linked to the war involving Iran. UAE officials said the decision reflects the country’s long-term economic and energy strategy and is intended to give Abu Dhabi more flexibility to respond to market conditions. Mazrouei told Reuters the UAE did not raise the issue with any other country. He said the move would not have a large immediate market impact because shipments through the Strait of Hormuz remain constrained. The Strait of Hormuz, between Iran and Oman, normally carries about one-fifth of global oil, and Gulf producers have struggled to ship exports through the route amid the disruption, reports based on Reuters said. SCMP reported that Brent had reached $111 a barrel and U.S. West Texas Intermediate was approaching $100 after trading near $70 and $65, respectively, before the war.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Cr4QapTbT-SOG5uWCJpXaM1ucrlMzolFXqIvOWHZH_OP4ya-NZqjg7vxGlCysKb8Kn5AWPNTSVr8xIleChQsobqygM7GnnF-wSGT_9xwbUhY' alt='Austrian police officers stand in front of the OPEC headquarters in Vienna, Austria, June 3, 2023.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Quota dispute: Abu Dhabi has objected for years to OPEC production quotas. In 2021, it refused to back an extension because its baseline was tied to 2018 output levels despite later investment.</li><li>Capacity gap: Semafor reported that OPEC granted the UAE a 3.5 million barrel-per-day baseline in June 2024, while ADNOC capacity has risen to 4.85 million bpd with a 5 million bpd target for 2030.</li><li>Regional split: Analysts cited by The Guardian, Semafor and The New York Times linked the exit to long-running disagreements between the UAE and Saudi Arabia over quotas, regional leadership and Abu Dhabi’s push to set its own policy.</li></ul><blockquote>We thank OPEC and its member countries for decades of constructive cooperation - Suhail Mohamed al-Mazrouei</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>The Hindu: The outlet noted that the move followed UAE criticism of fellow Arab states for not doing enough to protect it from Iranian attacks during the war. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/uae-leaves-opec-and-opec-in-huge-blow-to-global-oil-producers-group/article70916516.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Obaid Ahmed Al-Zaabi: The former UAE diplomat told RT that more countries are “very likely” to withdraw from OPEC and said the UAE’s departure had been “a long time coming.” (<a href='https://www.rt.com/news/639229-uae-opec-cartel-withdrawal/'>RT</a>)</li><li>AllSides: The report emphasized the rise of American oil output as a structural challenge to OPEC’s leverage, separate from the UAE decision. (<a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-04-28-0700/middle-east-uaes-opec-exit-deals-major-blow-cartel-amid-middle-east-oil-squeeze'>AllSides</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/4/29/uae-quits-opec-as-oil-cartel-takes-blow-during-war-on-iran'>UAE quits OPEC as oil cartel takes blow during war on Iran</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://allafrica.com/stories/202604290065.html'>Nigeria: UAE Withdraws From Opec, Opec+</a> - allafrica.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/week-asia/economics/article/3351776/why-uaes-exit-opec-good-news-asias-energy-security'>Why UAE’s exit from Opec is good news for Asia’s energy security</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/apr/28/how-uae-leave-opec-recast-middle-east-saudi-arabia-us'>How the UAE’s decision to leave Opec could recast the